{"step_index":86,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:16:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-15T23:46:54+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-15T23:46:59+05:30\nThe search for \"VideoScribe API CLI headless automation terminal script batch rendering\" returned the following summary:\nVideoScribe does not provide an official headless API or command-line interface (CLI) for automated, server-side, or batch rendering[1][2]. It is designed as a desktop-based, GUI-centric application[3][2], meaning projects must be opened and rendered manually within the application’s interface[4].\n\n### Key Considerations for Automation\n*   **No Headless Support:** Because VideoScribe requires a graphical user interface to load project files and process animations, it cannot be run in a terminal or headless server environment[3][2].\n*   **Manual Workflow:** Batch rendering (processing multiple projects without human intervention) is not a natively supported feature[4]. Rendering is performed locally on your machine and depends on the application's internal GUI processes[4][2].\n*   **Alternatives for Automation:** If your project requires programmatic video generation or large-scale batch rendering, you may need to look for \"headless\" video editing platforms[5][6]. These tools are built specifically for automation and provide APIs that allow you to send data (JSON) and receive rendered video files[5][6][7]. Common options in this space include:\n    *   **Remotion:** Excellent for code-first video generation using React[6].\n    *   **Shotstack / Creatomate / JSON2Video:** Cloud-based APIs designed for programmatic video editing and high-volume rendering[6].\n    *   **OpenCut:** An open-source alternative that supports a headless mode for batch rendering[8].\n\n### Workaround Strategy\nIf you are strictly tied to using VideoScribe, there is no official automation path[1]. Some users attempt to manage complex workflows by:\n1.  **Standardizing Assets:** Using VideoScribe's \"Default Settings\" to ensure consistency across projects, which reduces the manual setup time for each scribe[9].\n2.  **Post-Processing:** If you are building a larger video pipeline, you might use **FFmpeg** to stitch together or convert video files after they have been manually rendered from VideoScribe[10]. While this does not automate the rendering of the scribe itself, it is a standard command-line tool for automating the subsequent assembly and encoding of video assets[10].\n\nSources:\n[1] [apitracker.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFPE7vCpAsb9BWuijYmN7CIG85PJlELwNcSKySrqRWXyD_fUHpVt1BRywlU5Aa6hmG-N8oFqBR2wB1GXTe66pbBqLsCJE8JxgG80BWLJWXoctuFZBKCOKf4)\n[2] [pexo.ai](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHIOO-fLwCcjc_DkyBdv33Rw_zrG5ACUqOy5lMx0mgGBpUdyl3AhJr3peSv4-kB9uFClR0sEAKeBuHlLt6OSzj9LBsQqsXGorFgPgsMmRoHQNUedDALvGjyoXi4tXv_NsM_)\n[3] [videoscribe.co](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHl2Sr-FR1T18dO8iqnKqc7U_In0whwZGwPgh4uvIb9gELShM1Gazjuw83AU5EPLbY3pRE33jASEdzGtL4d4r68nwpElZgPN_ESycfg49dMS4onKym44zTWoCSeZpIei9FwkG3UUf_ZST_if_qIL8xmi7n0RNpjYXwnbg==)\n[4] [videoscribe.co](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGseRnxYg4ip948r2WqxvLNmmjdTOH_A6rUTTzT-TO3uJTCtSvEjkgxmda1MKgeinyNWu21nqUmi0wNjp9ZJI_eigzWYZM6CgzSilnc7y45GwE04ZNpNOxhSJGzmWHL3r9_OSUHwNwb6HJWUw_jbUWpxiDSvw==)\n[5] [flux-1.ai](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH_X6oXJkuNMBsmAQC4jYz_DW_dF1dOY3MrCOQ1vY5-zYPpdiyZDhYRG1cNqEBn3o8VnLuHIM9ftKWc6N1TqKKh2dXfILMfhz0dwnzcLJEZrB4s1LkOyH2y8ZKCzKsjH01uNCjmbJ4MTHtUkUQ=)\n[6] [wireflow.ai](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFd2lE73rgDbS9mhA-p6F2eoA6Ys0Qie6LRSCNq69t-yLWX_MTN6RA67VsYtRV4J-KDEpXW91GUFUqAHYPf2gPhQfQ1P7hpCl1x5wuOr3C1dS_pxkjuiB09I7YQ44ITbfN8g2GfaCfbmXlVSqvKUppbjG3QI5XKdaLZDEXvT_dSuobI2ty-BrxTuw==)\n[7] [itsrapid.ai](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEva8qCtR12MMAw48hKofWIbmait24rX3Q5TNXhyOZLDfByiDC9o3_jfXSyqeM2aldL4afSKfD7gFR7jWwpSsFDUwRWpLB9c_ZmdcxdK1bqIo4ee65WzUY3VEFjdZPR5umVSADGdw==)\n[8] [flux-1.ai](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH_X6oXJkuNMBsmAQC4jYz_DW_dF1dOY3MrCOQ1vY5-zYPpdiyZDhYRG1cNqEBn3o8VnLuHIM9ftKWc6N1TqKKh2dXfILMfhz0dwnzcLJEZrB4s1LkOyH2y8ZKCzKsjH01uNCjmbJ4MTHtUkUQ=)\n[9] [videoscribe.co](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFMdVJn6oTJMC0ceanmoyTnecm8oNzBOEpS10lIZOY7SkLXTcWSnlCfOegd87EIrH-lv1zRkRu7q--fL1lCb-KKdzkBtudEcel8chv95NF-C8XCkYlHqL9YOYW_d7CYNGt67p2qll8CL-NfxeXRLKdnsSN05pLlxSXgNwO2YJw71g==)\n[10] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGCzflLmw0wcQJ6jZYIVkIv5rI3NxsT0zrNwyuWPGr2kEq1cM_521WGeY79DqiP1KpDhUjWLSuKLHwh8S2vouQdfl5OOz3vzhbfeT-vmNHtb79p25igq8aUvFf8PQfd7vh9R6cGml-inpDoAr9pQriBaVJMt0R17Nxlj0dBdFnbqBpKcuARWzycptR6ExlqQAY_fRJPjw1Rt_scmnlGrQOa)"}
{"step_index":87,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:16:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-15T23:46:54+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-15T23:47:03+05:30\nThe search for \"open source whiteboard animation python github CLI tool\" returned the following summary:\nFor open-source whiteboard animation tools built with Python that include a CLI (Command Line Interface), you may find the following projects useful:\n\n### **Recommended CLI-Based Tools**\n\n*   **[whiteboard-animator](https://github.com/masihsultani/whiteboard-animator)**\n    *   **Description:** This is a Python library and CLI tool specifically designed to turn whiteboard-style images into hand-drawn reveal animations.\n    *   **Features:** It allows you to process images and audio to create MP4 videos, making it highly suitable for automated workflows. It is the render engine behind Kinoslide and supports narration syncing.\n    *   **Usage:** You can use simple commands like: `whiteboard-animate scene.png --duration 8 -o scene.mp4`.\n\n*   **[handanim](https://github.com/subroy13/handanim)**\n    *   **Description:** A Python library for programmatic animation in a human-style sketching format.\n    *   **Features:** It provides an intuitive Python API for creating scenes, animating geometric shapes, handwritten text, and visual explanations. It supports exporting to SVG or MP4.\n    *   **Target Use:** Ideal for educational videos, tutorials, and data storytelling where you want to script your animations in Python code.\n\n### **Other Notable Mentions**\n*   **[motionpicture](https://github.com/Sbozzolo/motionpicture):** A library that simplifies creating videos from individual frames. It includes a CLI utility (`mopi`) that allows you to configure your animation via command line or text files, providing a flexible way to turn Python code into video.\n*   **[ai-img2sketch](https://github.com/daslearning-org/ai-img2sketch):** While it uses a Gradio UI, it is a Python-based project that generates whiteboard sketch animations from uploaded images using OpenCV and NumPy, which can be useful if you prefer a local, non-AI-heavy approach.\n\n### **Summary of Approaches**\n| Tool | Primary Interface | Best For |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **whiteboard-animator** | CLI | Automating reveal animations from images/audio |\n| **handanim** | Python Library | Programmatically scripting educational animations |\n| **motionpicture** | CLI / Code | Generating frame-based animations from Python scripts |\n\nWhen setting these up, ensure you have **FFmpeg** installed on your system, as most of these tools rely on it for video encoding and processing."}
{"step_index":88,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:16:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-15T23:46:54+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-15T23:47:08+05:30\nThe search for \"Remotion whiteboard animation hand drawing SVG effect\" returned the following summary:\nTo create a whiteboard \"hand drawing\" effect in Remotion, you can leverage SVG path animation techniques by manipulating the `stroke-dasharray` and `stroke-dashoffset` CSS properties, or by using the `@remotion/paths` library for more advanced control.\n\n### Core Technique: Path Drawing\nThe classic \"drawing\" animation relies on SVG paths. By setting the `stroke-dasharray` to the total length of the path and gradually decreasing the `stroke-dashoffset` from that full length to zero, you create the effect of the line being drawn over time.\n\n### Implementation Steps\n\n1.  **Prepare Your SVG**:\n    *   Ensure your SVG paths have `fill=\"none\"` and a defined `stroke` and `stroke-width`.\n    *   Import your SVG into your Remotion component. You can clean up the SVG code by removing unnecessary attributes.\n\n2.  **Use `@remotion/paths`**:\n    *   Remotion provides a dedicated package to handle SVG path calculations, which simplifies the math required for these animations.\n    *   Install it via: `npm i @remotion/paths`\n    *   Key functions include:\n        *   `getLength()`: To find the total length of your SVG path.\n        *   `evolvePath()`: A powerful utility that can help animate the path drawing automatically based on the current frame and progress.\n\n3.  **Animate with `useCurrentFrame`**:\n    *   Use the `useCurrentFrame` hook to track the progress of your animation.\n    *   Combine this with the `interpolate` function to map the current frame range to the stroke offset values.\n\n### Example Workflow\n```tsx\nimport { useCurrentFrame, interpolate, spring, useVideoConfig } from \"remotion\";\nimport { getLength, evolvePath } from \"@remotion/paths\";\n\nexport const WhiteboardDrawing = ({ path }) => {\n  const frame = useCurrentFrame();\n  const { fps } = useVideoConfig();\n  \n  // Calculate the drawing progress (0 to 1)\n  const progress = interpolate(frame, [0, 60], [0, 1], { extrapolateRight: \"clamp\" });\n  \n  // Use evolvePath to get the path segment for the current progress\n  const animatedPath = evolvePath(progress, path);\n\n  return (\n    <svg>\n      <path d={animatedPath} stroke=\"black\" strokeWidth=\"2\" fill=\"none\" />\n    </svg>\n  );\n};\n```\n\n### Tips for a Realistic Effect\n*   **Hand-Drawn Look**: Use \"wiggle\" or \"rough\" path filters if you want a more organic, human-like line quality rather than perfect digital lines.\n*   **Staggered Animation**: If your SVG has multiple elements, use staggered delays for each path to make it look like a hand is moving between different parts of the drawing.\n*   **Add a \"Hand\" Cursor**: To complete the whiteboard effect, place an image or icon of a hand/pencil at the end of the current path segment. You can use `getPointAtLength()` from `@remotion/paths` to calculate the exact X/Y coordinates of the pen tip for each frame and position your hand element accordingly."}
{"step_index":89,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:17:08Z","content":"Priyanshu, \n\n### ANSWER\nYou cannot use VideoScribe via the terminal. It is a strictly manual, desktop-based graphical application with zero headless API, CLI, or automated batch rendering support. \n\nHowever, since you already use Remotion and FFmpeg, you can bypass VideoScribe entirely. The specific visual effect you want is called SVG path drawing, and you can build an automated, terminal-controlled version of it using Remotion's `@remotion/paths` library or specific open-source Python CLI tools.\n\n### WHY\n\n**The VideoScribe limitation:** \nVideoScribe is designed for non-technical users. It requires a human to click, drag, set camera positions, and manually click \"Render.\" There is no way to send it a JSON payload or script to output a video automatically.\n\n**How to automate this yourself (The Expert Path):**\n\n**1. Remotion (The best option since you know it)**\nThe \"hand holding a marker\" whiteboard effect is just basic geometry and masking. Here is the exact technical workflow:\n* Import an SVG file. Ensure it has `fill=\"none\"` and defined strokes.\n* Install `@remotion/paths`.\n* Use `getLength()` to calculate the total length of the vector lines.\n* Use `evolvePath()` with an interpolated frame count to reveal the SVG line frame-by-frame.\n* To add the \"hand\": Use the `getPointAtLength()` function. This gives you the exact X,Y pixel coordinates of the vector line at any given frame. You attach a PNG image of a hand to those coordinates, and the hand will perfectly follow the line as it draws.\n\n**2. Open-Source Python CLI Tools**\nIf you want ready-made terminal tools instead of coding it in React:\n* **`whiteboard-animator`** (by masihsultani on GitHub): A CLI tool built specifically for this. It takes a static image, applies a hand-drawn reveal mask, syncs it to an audio file, and outputs an MP4 using FFmpeg. \n  * Usage: `whiteboard-animate scene.png --duration 8 -o scene.mp4`\n* **`handanim`** (by subroy13 on GitHub): A Python library explicitly designed for programmatic animation of human-style sketching, targeted at educational videos. \n\n### COUNTER\n\nThe programmatic approach (Remotion/Python) gives you the speed and automation you want, but it introduces a massive asset problem. \n\nVideoScribe is successful because they pay human artists to manually define the \"draw paths\" inside tens of thousands of proprietary SVGs. When a human draws a house, they draw the frame, then the roof, then the windows. When you feed a random stock SVG into Remotion or a Python tool, the computer traces the raw mathematical vector coordinates. It might draw the window first, then a random line on the roof, then jump to the door. It looks chaotic, not human. \n\nTo make automated whiteboard animation look like an expert did it, you will have to manually clean up and re-order the vector paths inside Adobe Illustrator or Inkscape for every new graphic before feeding it to your script.\n\n### CONDITIONS\n\nBuilding a CLI-driven whiteboard animation pipeline using Remotion or Python is the correct decision **ONLY IF** your channel relies on a reusable library of standard assets (charts, text generation, generic icons) that you prepare once. \n\nIf your format requires unique, highly detailed custom illustrations for every single video, the programmatic approach will fail. The time you save on automated rendering will be completely lost debugging SVG coordinates to make the hand draw them logically. In that scenario, paying for a subscription to a UI tool like Animaker (which has an AI prompt-to-whiteboard generator) is the more realistic path for volume.\n\n### SOURCES\n* VideoScribe Documentation (verified lack of API/CLI support)\n* Remotion Documentation (`@remotion/paths` API)\n* GitHub: `masihsultani/whiteboard-animator`\n* GitHub: `subroy13/handanim`","thinking":"**Investigating VideoScribe Options**\n\nI'm delving into VideoScribe's functionality and internal workings, focusing on terminal interaction possibilities. I'm exploring ways to integrate it with Remotion and FFmpeg for automation. My focus is now on identifying and assessing any comparable open-source solutions or repos that could achieve similar results efficiently.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Options**\n\nI've synthesized the findings and identified some key limitations. VideoScribe is decidedly manual; there's no headless operation. However, I've unearthed several open-source alternatives. I'm especially keen on `whiteboard-animator` and `handanim`, Python-based tools, and also considering Manim for broader programmatic animation, plus confirming the Remotion route, which I see can do the drawing effect.\n\n\n**Confirming No CLI Access**\n\nI've confirmed VideoScribe's limitations. It lacks terminal access, relying solely on its GUI. However, Priyanshu notes that Remotion offers a viable alternative for programmatic recreation of the drawing effect, if that is necessary for you. Alternatively, `whiteboard-animator`, and other open-source Python CLIs may work, if not the manual SVG path creation in Remotion.\n\n\n**Evaluating Open Source Options**\n\nI'm now prioritizing programmatic solutions, particularly Remotion and Python CLIs. I've re-evaluated Remotion, recognizing its strength lies in SVG animation and path manipulation. Specifically, using `getLength()` and `getPointAtLength()` within Remotion is the key to automating the hand-drawn effect. I'm also deeply considering `whiteboard-animator` due to its straightforward CLI-based workflow and FFmpeg integration. I also think `handanim` could be useful. The goal remains batch rendering.\n\n\n**Assessing Path Data**\n\nI'm now grappling with the trade-off. The real hurdle is replicating VideoScribe's pre-configured SVG \"draw paths\". Standard SVGs drawn programmatically often look unnatural; a lot of clean-up will be required. Automating with Remotion or Python is efficient for predictable, high-volume content with clean assets, like text and charts. However, complex or varied illustrations would be better done manually.\n\n\n"}
