{"step_index":107,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:24:26Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\n### ANSWER\nHere is the master list of every tool we have uncovered in our research, categorized by exactly what they do. \n\nTo help you out based on your specific goals, I have separated them into two distinct tech stacks: the **Nitish Rajput Geopolitics Stack** (cinematic graphics) and the **Atomic Habits Whiteboard Stack** (vector sketching), along with the open-source and AI tools that power them.\n\n### WHY\n\n#### 1. The \"Nitish Rajput\" Style Stack (Cinematic & Geopolitics)\n*   **Adobe After Effects:** The heavy-duty animation software used to create the smooth, cinematic motion graphics, parallax photo effects, and moving text seen in Nitish Rajput's videos.\n*   **GeoLayers 3 (After Effects Plugin):** The specific plugin used to generate the high-quality, zooming 3D maps that are mandatory for geopolitical channels.\n*   **Adobe Premiere Pro / DaVinci Resolve:** Timeline editors used to cut the final video together, add sound effects, and splice in B-roll footage. DaVinci is the free alternative.\n\n#### 2. The \"Atomic Habits\" Style Stack (Commercial Whiteboard GUIs)\n*   **VideoScribe:** The exact paid software used to create the Atomic Habits video you had me analyze. It auto-animates a human hand drawing SVG vectors on a white canvas.\n*   **Doodly:** A budget-friendly, one-time-purchase alternative to VideoScribe with slightly fewer features but the exact same hand-drawing aesthetic.\n*   **Explaindio:** Another commercial GUI software that mixes whiteboard sketching with standard 2D animation.\n\n#### 3. The Open-Source / CLI Automated Stack (GitHub Alternatives)\n*   **Remotion:** A React-based framework that lets you generate video using code. By using the `@remotion/paths` library, you can programmatically calculate SVG coordinates and attach a PNG hand to automate the VideoScribe effect via CLI.\n*   **`whiteboard-video-engine` (Python):** A headless CLI engine that takes SVGs and text via terminal commands and renders them into whiteboard animations using FFmpeg.\n*   **`whiteboard-animator` (Python):** A CLI tool that takes a static image (like a chart or drawing) and converts it into a video showing that image being drawn stroke-by-stroke, synced to audio.\n*   **`handanim` (Python):** A coding library for building mathematical or educational sketches from scratch, outputting video with sketchy, human-drawn fills.\n*   **`Inkplainer-OS` (Web):** A completely free, open-source browser-based clone of VideoScribe (features a GUI, not CLI).\n*   **`GreaseWriter` (Blender):** A Blender Python add-on that physically simulates human writing physics for text, complete with organic pauses between pen strokes.\n\n#### 4. The AI Automation Layer (For your Faceless Workflow)\n*   **ElevenLabs:** The absolute best text-to-speech AI for generating hyper-realistic English and Hindi voiceovers.\n*   **Suno:** Generates custom background music based on text prompts to lay underneath your narration.\n*   **Envato Elements / Freepik:** Massive libraries where you download the raw SVG graphics, photos, and B-roll to feed into your animation tools.\n\n### COUNTER\n\nYou asked me to help you based on *both* the Nitish Rajput channel AND the whiteboard video you had me analyze. The harsh truth is that these two references represent fundamentally opposing production styles. \n\nYou cannot build a Nitish Rajput-level geopolitical channel using open-source whiteboard scripts or VideoScribe. Geopolitics requires maps, archival footage, and serious cinematic tones. Conversely, you do not use After Effects and GeoLayers to summarize *Atomic Habits* with stickmen. \n\n### CONDITIONS\n\nBefore you pick a tool to learn or automate, you must make the final decision on your niche. \n*   **If you choose Geopolitics:** You must abandon the whiteboard automation idea and commit to learning After Effects + Premiere. \n*   **If you choose Business/Book Summaries (15-20 min, 6 times a month):** You must abandon the Nitish Rajput cinematic style and commit to building an automated pipeline using Remotion or `whiteboard-video-engine` fed by ElevenLabs and Freepik SVGs.","thinking":"**Begin Tool Listing**\n\nI'm starting to list all the tools I've identified throughout this process. I'm focusing on those found in the initial session, including the channel analysis and GitHub/CLI research I did. I'm aiming for a comprehensive list.\n\n\n**Categorizing Tools for Nitish**\n\nI'm now categorizing the listed tools based on their relevance to Nitish Rajput's style and the \"Atomic Habits\" explainer. I'm focusing on Adobe After Effects and GeoLayers 3, which seem central to the channel's look. I will also be considering ElevenLabs for voiceovers and how the video editing and asset prep tools fit the \"Atomic Habits\" whiteboard style.\n\n\n**Expanding Tool List**\n\nI've expanded the tool list to include specific software identified from the \"Atomic Habits\" video analysis. VideoScribe is the main GUI used, with Doodly as a budget option. Explaindio/Vyond are also considered. I found DaVinci Resolve as a good editing alternative to Premiere. Finally, I've added Suno for AI-generated music.\n\nI've also researched several GitHub tools. Remotion and `@remotion/paths` are used with React for programmatic video generation, while whiteboard-video-engine and whiteboard-animator are CLI alternatives. I will also incorporate handanim as an option.\n\n\n**Adding New Tools**\n\nPriyanshu, I've just integrated new tools into the list based on the specifics you've provided. I've added GreaseWriter for hand-drawn text, Inkplainer-OS as a free VideoScribe alternative, and anything2explainer which is a Remotion-based explainer generator. I will now integrate this into the category of tools that are suitable for Atomic Habits videos.\n\n\n**Organizing Tools into Categories**\n\nI'm now organizing the tool list into four categories: Geopolitics, Commercial Whiteboard, Automated Whiteboard (CLI), and Universal Faceless tools. I've placed After Effects and GeoLayers 3 in the Geopolitics stack, while VideoScribe, Doodly, and Explaindio belong to the Commercial Whiteboard category. Remotion and `@remotion/paths` fit into the Automated stack.\n\n\n**Refining Workflow Choices**\n\nI'm now refining the tool list with your channel constraints in mind, Priyanshu. I've re-reviewed the GitHub tools like `whiteboard-video-engine`, `whiteboard-animator`, and `handanim`, and re-evaluated `Inkplainer-OS` as a VideoScribe alternative. I've also re-confirmed ElevenLabs and Suno, along with Envato Elements and Freepik. The core insight is: *Nitish can't make Nitish videos with these tools*. I now need to explicitly guide you through the decision.\n\n\n"}
